  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 300 PM EDT Wed Aug 19 2026\n\nThursday Night through Saturday Night...\n\nThe remnants of a sfc cold front should sink into southern KY and TN \nThursday night as weak high pressure moves into the Midwest and Ohio \nValley. While this should bring lower PWATs and deep layer moisture \ninto the region from the north, near-sfc moisture levels are \nexpected to remain elevated, with sfc dewpoints remaining in the \nupper 60s and low-to-mid 70s as we head into Friday. There may be \njust enough moisture across southern KY for additional rain chances \nThursday night, but probabilities remain low (15-20%) at this time. \nIf there is any clearing farther to the north, fog development would \nbe favored Thursday night given light winds and a moist boundary \nlayer, and there is already a modest signal on 12Z HREF guidance for \nfog Friday morning.\n\nFriday into Saturday, the remnants of the front should dissipate and \npush to the south as troughing continues across eastern portions of \nthe CONUS. Although the air mass should remain fairly muggy, limited \nforcing should keep most locations dry Friday into Friday night. A \nsecondary upper shortwave is expected to drop across Ontario and \ninto the northeast US Saturday into Saturday night, allowing a \nreinforcing cold front to approach the region from the north. While \nearlier model guidance suggested this system could bring a chance \nfor showers and storms, potentially even a few strong storms, recent \nguidance has trended farther to the east with the mean trough axis \npositioning. As a result, better chances for showers and storms \nshould remain to the northeast of the region Saturday into Saturday \nnight, with only isolated chances for showers and storms expected \nacross southern IN in the current forecast. Temperatures Friday into \nSaturday should be near to slightly below normals, with highs in the \nmid 80s and lows in the mid 60s.\n\nSunday through Early Next Week...\n\nAs the upper level pattern amplifies over the second half of the \nweekend, deepening troughing along the east coast should allow a \ncold front to push through the region Saturday night into Sunday. \nThis front shouldn't bring much in the way of a cool down; instead, \ndrier air will be the main feature of the post-frontal air mass, \nlimiting precipitation chances for Sunday into early next week. LREF \nguidance shows ensemble mean PWAT values below climatological norms \nSunday into Monday, with dewpoints in the upper 50s and low-to-mid \n60s returning to the area.\n\nAs we head deeper into next week, a NW flow pattern should continue \nacross the Ohio Valley, with ridging trying to extend into the \nPlains while troughing continues across the eastern seaboard. This \npattern could bring the potential for transient systems to ride \nalong the edge of the upper ridge, bringing a chance for showers and \nstorms, though at this time, the strongest signal in guidance for \nthese systems is to the southwest of the area.",
